\section{Themes}{


rblimp's plots draw with the colors, line widths, point sizes, and fonts of the plot's
ggplot2 theme, so adding a theme restyles the whole plot. Use any ggplot2 theme, or
\code{\link{theme_blimp}} for rblimp's ready-made look, and set one with
\code{\link[ggplot2:theme_set]{ggplot2::theme_set}} to use it for every plot in the session. Colors and sizes come
from the theme's geom settings: points and lines use the \code{ink} color and curves the
\code{accent} color.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{p + theme_blimp(base_size = 14)
p + ggplot2::theme(geom = ggplot2::element_geom(ink = "grey30", accent = "black"))

old <- ggplot2::theme_set(theme_blimp())  # every plot from here on
ggplot2::theme_set(old)                   # back to the previous theme
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{rblimp's own settings}{


A few things have no ggplot2 theme setting: the transparency of points and bands, the
data color (which \code{\link{theme_blimp}} uses for its points), category-mean markers,
reference lines, case labels, and Johnson-Neyman boundaries. Set them with
\code{\link{blimp_style}}. To show which cases had their outcome imputed, add \code{\link{scale_imputed}}
(or \code{\link{scale_imputed_manual}} to pick the colors). Titles and labels work as in any
ggplot; for a figure, you might drop the title and the "Averaged over M imputations"
subtitle.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{p + blimp_style(point_alpha = 0.1, reference_color = "red")
p + scale_imputed()
p + ggplot2::labs(title = NULL, subtitle = NULL)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Styling many plots at once}{


Keep a style in a list and add it like a single component. \code{\link{diagnostic_plots}} and
\code{\link{distribution_plots}} return named lists of plots, and \code{+} adds to every plot in the
list; pick plots by name, or keep the ones whose names match a pattern with \code{subset()}.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{my_style <- list(theme_blimp(base_size = 14), blimp_style(point_alpha = 0.1))
p + my_style

plots <- diagnostic_plots(model) + my_style
subset(plots, "index")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Saving}{


Save a plot as an image with \code{\link[ggplot2:ggsave]{ggplot2::ggsave}}, or a whole list with a loop. To keep
a plot to draw or change later, use \code{\link{saveRDS}}; it keeps only the data it draws, not
the model.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{ggplot2::ggsave("residuals.png", p + my_style, width = 6, height = 4, dpi = 300)

for (name in names(plots)) \{
    ggplot2::ggsave(paste0(name, ".png"), plots[[name]], width = 6, height = 4, dpi = 300)
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\examples{
\dontshow{if (has_blimp()) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
# A model with some missing outcome values
mydata <- rblimp_sim(
    c('x ~ normal(0, 1)', 'y ~ normal(10 + 0.5*x, 1)'),
    n = 100, seed = 981273
)
mydata$y[1:20] <- NA
model <- rblimp('y ~ x', mydata, nimps = 20, seed = 10972, burn = 1000, iter = 1000)
p <- residual_plot(model, 'y', x = 'x')

# A theme, rblimp's settings, and the imputed values marked
p + theme_blimp() + blimp_style(point_alpha = 0.2) + scale_imputed()

# One style for a whole list of plots
my_style <- list(theme_blimp(base_size = 14), blimp_style(point_alpha = 0.1))
plots <- diagnostic_plots(model) + my_style
subset(plots, "index")

# Save a plot
ggplot2::ggsave(file.path(tempdir(), "residuals.png"), p + my_style, width = 6, height = 4)
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
}
